Third-party terms for the cstar package
=======================================

MIT-licensed sources
--------------------

The proof libraries this package builds and links come from two MIT-licensed
repositories, pinned by content hash in dependency.json:

  cstar_examples  32d61571e20eeecd10a6ecd4f00592cb6618e524
                  proof/, userlib/qcp/, userlib/operational/,
                  tutorial/common.c, array/lib/
                  LICENSES/cstar_examples-MIT.txt
                  (sha256 1581b13c2150a8c96a82c4edc0a3a2f7b334186cfb4c86b65fb
                          bb0c6c3cdd610)

  cstar_stdlib    7e8662b8a0706b77409667aaa022346d56836453
                  the content of cstar_examples/proof/
                  LICENSES/cstar_stdlib-MIT.txt
                  (sha256 11dfe4974f3ceb6ae2c83444a7d2d8acddb58572fb7bcdb07d0
                          e15239a2c3df1)

proof/x2c_array_helpers.{h,c} is this package's own C* source. It is
written against the MIT headers above and compiled by cstarc; it copies no
upstream code.

The C* core release ships no license
------------------------------------

The pinned darwin-aarch64 v0.5.7 release
(sha256 f8fb646d20e9f59aa36f7cd39359acbeacb0f5fdbd4a4ce465112e432bcafd7f)
supplies cstarc, cst_clang, hol_light_server, libcstar.a, libsac.dylib, and
the C headers. Its source is closed and the archive contains no LICENSE file
and no stated terms.

packages/LICENSE-POLICY.md rejects unclear terms by default. Gary made the
explicit project decision on 2026-09-09 to proceed anyway, for an
experimental package that downloads the release into a local dependency
cache and redistributes none of it. That decision covers this package only.
